      "id": 1,
      "text": "Rock Hall was named for a white sandstone mansion and incorporated in 1908.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Hall%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally called Rock Hall Crossroads, the town is now a fishing, sailing and recreational boating hub on the Chesapeake Bay.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Hall%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A railroad almost reached Rock Hall in 1872 when the Kent County Railroad planned tracks from Massey through Chestertown, but the project stalled.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        6,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Hall%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Hinchingham and Trumpington, two historic homes, are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_Hall%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Kent County Public Library runs a branch right in Rock Hall, serving the local community.",
